5.1 COMPILING
=============

The wordset COMPILING contains words that compile *Note IMMEDIATE::
words and numbers.  You need special precautions because these words
would execute during compilation. Numbers are compiled "in line"  ,
behind a word that fetches them.

* Menu:



* COMPILE  ::
* DLITERAL  ::
* LITERAL  ::
* LIT  ::
* [COMPILE]  ::


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: COMPILE,  Next: DLITERAL,  Prev: COMPILING,  Up: COMPILING

5.1.1 COMPILE
-------------

Name: COMPILE

   No stackeffect

   Attributes: C2

   Description: When the word containing `COMPILE'  executes, the
execution address of the word following `COMPILE'  is copied (compiled)
into the dictionary.  This allows specific compilation situations to be
handled in addition to simply compiling an execution address (which the
interpreter already does).

   See also: `[COMPILE]'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: DLITERAL,  Next: LITERAL,  Prev: COMPILE,  Up: COMPILING

5.1.2 DLITERAL
--------------

Name: DLITERAL

   Stackeffect: d -- d (executing) d -- (compiling)

   Attributes: P

   Description: If compiling, compile a stack double number into a
literal. Later execution of the definition containing the literal will
push it to the stack. If executing, the number will remain on the stack.


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: LITERAL,  Next: LIT,  Prev: DLITERAL,  Up: COMPILING

5.1.3 LITERAL
-------------

Name: LITERAL

   Stackeffect: n -- (compiling)

   Attributes: P,C2,L0

   Description: If compiling, then compile the stack value N as a 16
bit literal.  This definition is immediate so that it will execute
during a colon definition. The intended use is:      `: xxx    [
calculate ]  LITERAL  ;' Compilation is suspended for the compile time
calculation of a value.  Compilation is resumed and `LITERAL'  compiles
this value.


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: LIT,  Next: [COMPILE],  Prev: LITERAL,  Up: COMPILING

5.1.4 LIT
---------

Name: LIT

   Stackeffect: -- n

   Attributes: C2,L0

   Description: Within a colon-definition, `LIT'  is compiled followed
by a 16 bit literal number given during compilation. Later execution of
`LIT'  causes the contents of this next dictionary address to be pushed
to the stack.


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: [COMPILE],  Prev: LIT,  Up: COMPILING

5.1.5 [COMPILE]
---------------

Name: [COMPILE]

   No stackeffect

   Attributes: p,C

   Description: Used in a colon-definition in form:
`[COMPILE]'  will force the compilation of an immediate definitions,
that would otherwise execute during compilation. The above example will
select the `FORTH'  vocabulary then XXX executes, rather than at
compile time.

   See also: `COMPILE'


