5.20 STACKS
===========

The wordset STACKS contains words related to the "data stack"  and
"return stack" . Words can be moved between both stacks.  Stacks can be
reinitialised and the value used to initialise the "stack pointer" 's
can be altered.

* Menu:



* >R  ::
* R0  ::
* R>  ::
* RP!  ::
* RP@  :r_p_fetch
* R  ::
* S0  ::
* SP!  ::
* SP@  :s_p_fetch


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: >R,  Next: R0,  Prev: STACKS,  Up: STACKS

5.20.1 >R
---------

Name: >R

   Stackeffect: n --

   Attributes: C,L0

   Description: Remove a number from the computation stack and place as
the most accessable on the return stack. Use should be balanced with
`R>'  in the same definition.

   See also: `R'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: R0,  Next: R>,  Prev: >R,  Up: STACKS

5.20.2 R0
---------

Name: R0

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes: U

   Description: A user variable containing the initial location of the
return stack.

   See also: `RP!'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: R>,  Next: RP!,  Prev: R0,  Up: STACKS

5.20.3 R>
---------

Name: R>

   Stackeffect: -- n

   Attributes: L0

   Description: Remove the top value from the return stack and leave it
on the computation stack.

   See also: `>R' `R'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: RP!,  Next: r_p_fetch,  Prev: R>,  Up: STACKS

5.20.4 RP!
----------

Name: RP!

   No stackeffect

   Attributes:

   Description: Initialize the return stack pointer from user variable
`R0'  .


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: r_p_fetch,  Next: R,  Prev: RP!,  Up: STACKS

5.20.5 r_p_fetch
----------------

Name: RP@

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes:

   Description: Return the address ADDR of the current return stack
position, i.e. pointing the current topmost value.    (e.g.  `1 >R  RP@
@  . R>'     would    type `1' )

   See also: `S0' `SP!'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: R,  Next: S0,  Prev: r_p_fetch,  Up: STACKS

5.20.6 R
--------

Name: R

   Stackeffect: -- n

   Attributes:

   Description: Copy the top of the return stack to the computation
stack.

   See also: `>R'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: S0,  Next: SP!,  Prev: R,  Up: STACKS

5.20.7 S0
---------

Name: S0

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes: U

   Description: A user variable that contains the initial value for the
data stack pointer.

   See also: `SP!'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: SP!,  Next: s_p_fetch,  Prev: S0,  Up: STACKS

5.20.8 SP!
----------

Name: SP!

   No stackeffect

   Attributes:

   Description: Initialize the stack pointer from `S0'  .


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: s_p_fetch,  Prev: SP!,  Up: STACKS

5.20.9 s_p_fetch
----------------

Name: SP@

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes:

   Description: Return the address ADDR of the stack position, as it
was before `SP@'  was executed.    (e.g.  `1  2  SP@  @  ...'     would
  type `2  2   1' )

   See also: `S0' `SP!'


