5.21.7 BLOCK-HANDLE
-------------------

Name: BLOCK-HANDLE

   Stackeffect: --n

   Attributes:

   Description: Leave a file handle in N .  If it is negative there is
no block file open, otherwise the handle is used by the system to
access blocks.

   See also: `BLOCK-FILE' `BLOCK-INIT' `BLOCK-EXIT'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: BLOCK-INIT,  Next: BLOCK,  Prev: BLOCK-HANDLE,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.8 BLOCK-INIT
-----------------

Name: BLOCK-INIT

   Stackeffect: --

   Attributes:

   Description: Map the blocks on the block file `BLOCK-FILE' , i.e.
the mass storage words refer to the blocks in this file, using
BLOCK-HANDLE to acces it.

   See also: `BLOCK' `LIST' `LOAD' `BLOCK-EXIT'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: BLOCK,  Next: BUFFER,  Prev: BLOCK-INIT,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.9 BLOCK
------------

Name: BLOCK

   Stackeffect: n -- addr

   Attributes: ISO FIG L0

   Description: Leave the memory address of the block buffer containing
block N, which is the physical disk block `OFFSET+n'.  If the block is
not already in memory, it is transferred from disc to which ever buffer
was least recently written. If the block occupying that buffer has been
marked as updated, it is rewritten to disc before block N is read into
the buffer.  In this experimental version `PMASK' restricts the number
of blocks to 0FFH.

   See also: `BUFFER' `R/W' `OFFSET' `UPDATE' `FLUSH'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: BUFFER,  Next: DISK-ERR,  Prev: BLOCK,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.10 BUFFER
--------------

Name: BUFFER

   Stackeffect: n -- addr

   Attributes: ISO FIG

   Description: Obtain the next memory buffer, assigning it to block N .
If the contents of the buffer is marked as updated, it is written to
the disc. The block is not read from the disc. The address left is the
first cell within the buffer for data storage.

   See also: `BLOCK' `USE' `PREV'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: DISK-ERR,  Next: EMPTY-BUFFERS,  Prev: BUFFER,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.11 DISK-ERR
----------------

Name: DISK-ERR

   Stackeffect: u--

   Attributes:

   Description: Interpret U as the status of a disk i/o BDOS call.
Report an error if it contains an error condition.

   See also: `BLOCK-FILE' `BLOCK-HANDLE' `BLOCK-INIT' `BLOCK-EXIT'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: EMPTY-BUFFERS,  Next: FIRST,  Prev: DISK-ERR,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.12 EMPTY-BUFFERS
---------------------

Name: EMPTY-BUFFERS

   No stackeffect

   Attributes: ISO FIG L0

   Description: Mark all block-buffers as empty, not necessarily
affecting the contents. Updated blocks are not written to the disc.
This is also an initialization proceedure before first use of the disc.

   See also: `FLUSH' `BLOCK' `SCREEN'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: FIRST,  Next: FLUSH,  Prev: EMPTY-BUFFERS,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.13 FIRST
-------------

Name: FIRST

   Stackeffect: -- n

   Attributes:

   Description: A constant that leaves the address of the first
(lowest) block buffer.  This is allocated in the lowest 1 Megabyte of
memory, to allow the BIOS to access it.

   See also: `BLOCK' `LIMIT'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: FLUSH,  Next: LIMIT,  Prev: FIRST,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.14 FLUSH
-------------

Name: FLUSH

   No stackeffect

   Attributes: ISO FIG L0

   Description: Transfer the content of each UPDATE d block buffer to
disk.  They are no longer associated with a block and their content is
no longer available.

   See also: `EMPTY-BUFFERS' `BLOCK' `SCREEN'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: LIMIT,  Next: OFFSET,  Prev: FLUSH,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.15 LIMIT
-------------

Name: LIMIT

   Stackeffect: --- n

   Attributes:

   Description: A constant leaving the address just above the highest
memory available for a disc buffer.

   See also: `BLOCK' `FIRST'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: OFFSET,  Next: PREV,  Prev: LIMIT,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.16 OFFSET
--------------

Name: OFFSET

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes: U

   Description: A user variable which may contain a block offset to
disc drives, such as to free part of it for boot-code.  As blocks are
allocated in a file on the host operating system it is 0.

   The contents of `OFFSET'  is added to the stack number by `BLOCK'  before
calling  R/W .  Messages by `MESSAGE'  are independent of `OFFSET'  .

   See also: `BLOCK' `MESSAGE '


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: PREV,  Next: R/W,  Prev: OFFSET,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.17 PREV
------------

Name: PREV

   Stackeffect: --- addr

   Attributes:

   Description: A variable containing the address of the disc buffer
most recently referenced. The `UPDATE'  command marks this buffer to be
later written to disc.

   See also: `BLOCK'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: R/W,  Next: SEEK,  Prev: PREV,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.18 R/W
-----------

Name: R/W

   Stackeffect: addr blk f -

   Attributes:

   Description: The fig-FORTH standard disc read-write linkage. ADDR
specifies the source or destination block buffer, BLK is the sequential
number of the referenced physical block; and F is a flag for F=0 write
and F=l read. `R/W'  determines the location on mass storage, performs
the read-write and aborts on errors.

   See also: `BLOCK'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: SEEK,  Next: UPDATE,  Prev: R/W,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.19 SEEK
------------

Name: SEEK

   Stackeffect: n--

   Attributes:

   Description: A block file must have been opened by `BLOCK-INIT'  .
Position the file pointer at block N in behalf of subsequent reads and
writes.

   See also: `BLOCK' `LIST' `LOAD'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: UPDATE,  Next: USE,  Prev: SEEK,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.20 UPDATE
--------------

Name: UPDATE

   No stackeffect

   Attributes: L0

   Description: Marks the most recently referenced block (pointed to by
`PREV'  ) as altered. The block will subsequently be transferred
automatically to disc should its buffer be required for storage of a
different block.

   See also: `BLOCK' `SCREEN'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: USE,  Prev: UPDATE,  Up: STORAGE

5.21.21 USE
-----------

Name: USE

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes:

   Description: A variable containing the address of the block buffer
to use next, as the least recently written.

   See also: `BLOCK'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: STRING,  Next: SUPERFLUOUS,  Prev: STORAGE,  Up: Glossary

5.22 STRING
===========

The wordset STRING contains words that manipulate strings of characters.

* Menu:



* -TRAILING  ::
* BL  ::
* COUNT  ::
* MATCH  ::


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: -TRAILING,  Next: BL,  Prev: STRING,  Up: STRING

5.22.1 -TRAILING
----------------

Name: -TRAILING

   Stackeffect: addr n1 -- addr n2

   Attributes:

   Description: Adjusts the character count N1 of a text string
beginning at address ADDR so as not to contain trailing blanks.


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: BL,  Next: COUNT,  Prev: -TRAILING,  Up: STRING

5.22.2 BL
---------

Name: BL

   Stackeffect: -- c

   Attributes:

   Description:  A constant that leaves the    ascii value for "blank".


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: COUNT,  Next: MATCH,  Prev: BL,  Up: STRING

5.22.3 COUNT
------------

Name: COUNT

   Stackeffect: addr1 -- addr2 n

   Attributes: L0

   Description: Leave the byte address ADDR2 and byte count N of a
message text beginning at address ADDR1 . It is presumed that the first
byte at ADDR1 contains the text byte count and the actual text starts
with the second byte.

   See also: `TYPE'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: MATCH,  Prev: COUNT,  Up: STRING

5.22.4 MATCH
------------

Name: MATCH

   Stackeffect: add1 n1 addr2 n2 - f o

   Attributes:

   Description: This word supposedly compares strings.  Its
documentation looked like this

      ( cursor:addr   byte:left   str:addr  str:len
                ---   flag  new:cursor:offset         )

      This version of MATCH will handle string lengths
           up to 65535 bytes in length.
I hope I interpreted this stack diagram right.


