5.24 SYSTEM
===========

The wordset SYSTEM contains words that call the underlying operating
system or functions available in the BIOS-rom.

* Menu:



* BDOS  ::
* BIOS  ::
* BYE  ::


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: BDOS,  Next: BIOS,  Prev: SYSTEM,  Up: SYSTEM

5.24.1 BDOS
-----------

Name: BDOS

   Stackeffect: a b c d i--ar br cr dr fl

   Attributes:

   Description: Do a call of "BDOS" interrupt (21H) with `a b c d' in
registers `AX BX CX DX'. Upon return those registers contains `ar br cr
dr' and `fl' is the content of the processors flag register. This draws
upon all facilities made available by MSDOS.

   See also: `BIOS'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: BIOS,  Next: BYE,  Prev: BDOS,  Up: SYSTEM

5.24.2 BIOS
-----------

Name: BIOS

   Stackeffect: a b c d i--ar br cr dr fl

   Attributes:

   Description: Do a call of "BIOS" interrupt I with `a b c d' in
registers `AX BX CX DX'. Upon return those registers contains `ar br cr
dr' and `fl' is the content of the processors flag register.

   See also: `BDOS'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: BYE,  Prev: BIOS,  Up: SYSTEM

5.24.3 BYE
----------

Name: BYE

   Stackeffect: --

   Attributes: ISO FIG

   Description: Switch back to real mode.  Return to the host
environment MSDOS.

   See also: `COLD'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: VOCABULARIES,  Prev: SYSTEM,  Up: Glossary

5.25 VOCABULARIES
=================

The dictionary is subdivided in non-overlapping subsets: the
"vocabulary" 's. *Note DICTIONARY::.  They are created by the defining
word `VOCABULARY'  and filled by defining words while that vocabulary
is `CURRENT'  . They regulate how words are found, different
vocabularies can have words with the same names.  The wordset
VOCABULARIES contains words that manipulate those vocabularies.

* Menu:



* CONTEXT  ::
* CURRENT  ::
* DEFINITIONS  ::
* FORTH  ::
* LATEST  ::
* VOC-LINK  ::


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: CONTEXT,  Next: CURRENT,  Prev: VOCABULARIES,  Up: VOCABULARIES

5.25.1 CONTEXT
--------------

Name: CONTEXT

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes: U,L0

   Description: A user variable containing a pointer to the vocabulary
within which dictionary searches will first begin.

   See also: `VOCABULARY' `CURRENT'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: CURRENT,  Next: DEFINITIONS,  Prev: CONTEXT,  Up: VOCABULARIES

5.25.2 CURRENT
--------------

Name: CURRENT

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes: U,L0

   Description: A user variable containing a pointer to the vocabulary
to which new words will be added.

   See also: `VOCABULARY' `CONTEXT'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: DEFINITIONS,  Next: FORTH,  Prev: CURRENT,  Up: VOCABULARIES

5.25.3 DEFINITIONS
------------------

Name: DEFINITIONS

   No stackeffect

   Attributes: L1

   Description: Used in the form:            CCCC `DEFINITIONS' 

   Set the `CURRENT'  vocabulary to the CONTEXT vocabulary. In the
example, executing vocabulary name CCCC made it the `CONTEXT' 

   vocabulary and executing `DEFINITIONS'  made both specify vocabulary
CCCC .

   See also: `VOCABULARY'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: FORTH,  Next: LATEST,  Prev: DEFINITIONS,  Up: VOCABULARIES

5.25.4 FORTH
------------

Name: FORTH

   No stackeffect

   Attributes: P,L1

   Description: The name of the primary vocabulary.  Execution makes
`FORTH'  the `CONTEXT'  vocabulary. Until additional user vocabularies
are defined, new user definitions become a part of `FORTH'  . `FORTH'  is
immediate, so it will execute during the creation of a
colon-definition, to select this vocabulary at compile time.

   See also: `VOCABULARY'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: LATEST,  Next: VOC-LINK,  Prev: FORTH,  Up: VOCABULARIES

5.25.5 LATEST
-------------

Name: LATEST

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes:

   Description: Leave the name field address ( "NFA"  ) of the topmost
word in the `CURRENT' 

   vocabulary.

   See also: `VOCABULARY'


File: fig86.msdos32.info,  Node: VOC-LINK,  Prev: LATEST,  Up: VOCABULARIES

5.25.6 VOC-LINK
---------------

Name: VOC-LINK

   Stackeffect: -- addr

   Attributes: U

   Description: A user variable containing the address of the DLFA in
the word most recently created by `VOCABULARY'  . All vocabulary names
are linked by these fields to allow control for `FORGET'  ting thru
multiple vocabularies.

   See also: `VOCABULARY'


